fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1050", description: some(value: "CHECK A [simp]/[unfold] RULE'S EFFECT ROWS AFTER EFFECT SUBSTITUTION, NOT AT THE DECLARATION. Today the formation gate (check_simp_effectful_ops -> effect_row_blocking_equations) reads the operation's DECLARED row. For an effect-POLYMORPHIC operation that row is a VARIABLE, so the gate refuses laws that are valid at every carrier the corpus actually has.\n\nMEASURED, driving proposal 054's own example on the real stdlib (rebuild required — the CLI embeds the stdlib via include_str!, so an edit without `cargo build` is invisible):\n\n    -- in sort anthill.prelude.PersistentCollection\n    rule isEmpty(insert(?c, ?x)) <=> false          -- LOADS CLEAN (inert law)\n    rule isEmpty(insert(?c, ?x)) <=> false [simp]   -- REFUSED, twice\n\nRefused naming `PersistentCollection.insert` (row {Effect}) and `Iterable.isEmpty` (row {E}). NEITHER IS EFFECTFUL: `effects Effect = ?` / `effects E = ?` declare row VARIABLES, and list.anthill documents its provision as PURE — 'List satisfies PersistentCollection (pure). Effect left unbound by design ... grounded to {} at the consumption boundary by the typer (WI-357 concrete carrier; WI-365 abstract self-receiver)'.\n\nWHAT WAS ALREADY FIXED, so this ticket is not about wording: WI-1049 taught the predicate to tell the two cases apart (`EquationBlock::Effectful` vs `::Polymorphic`) and the message now says 'is effect-POLYMORPHIC — its row {Effect} is a row VARIABLE, not an effect', and points at the repair. The VERDICT is unchanged. This ticket is about the verdict.\n\nWHY THE CURRENT VERDICT IS DEFENSIBLE — state it before changing it. A rule declared on the SPEC binds EVERY carrier, including ones not yet written. Nothing stops a carrier providing an effectful `insert` (one that logs, or allocates through External). Then the rewrite DROPS the matched call and the effect runs zero times instead of once. At the declaration site the row is genuinely unknown, so refusing is sound. `effect_row_blocking_equations`' doc has always said the polymorphic case is over-refused KNOWINGLY.\n\nTHE PROPOSAL: decide the gate on the SUBSTITUTED row. The ingredient exists — [simp] already requires a STATICALLY KNOWN CARRIER to fire (set.anthill: laws fire 'once the redex's carrier is known to provide Set'), and with the carrier known the spec op's row sigma-instantiates. A rewrite whose instantiated row is {} is sound; one whose row is non-empty is declined, at that redex.\n\n054 HAS A STATED POSITION AGAINST THIS AND IT MUST BE ANSWERED, NOT IGNORED — 'the simp rewriter (fire_simp) checks no effect rows at the firing site — sound today only because effectful ops never *become* simp equations (formation is gated as above). The one real hole is a user-written [simp]-tagged equation rule whose sides mention an External-rowed operation symbol ... The gate for that belongs at load-time tag validation, not the firing site.' NOTE ITS SCOPE: that argument is about an op whose row is CONCRETELY External. The row-VARIABLE case is not discussed in 054 at all; the over-refusal is admitted only in a Rust doc comment. So this is not a reversal of 054's reasoning — it is the case 054 did not consider. Say which in the delivery.\n\nTHE HAZARD TO DESIGN AGAINST, explicitly: moving a check from formation to firing turns one loud load-time refusal into a per-redex decision, and a decision not to fire is SILENT. An author who writes the law expecting it to apply on an effectful carrier would get no rewrite and no diagnostic — the silent-skip the repo forbids. Whatever form this takes must keep a loud channel for 'this redex declined and here is why'.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT WORK, measured, so nobody re-tries it: binding the row at the PROVISION does not help. `fact PersistentCollection[List[T], T, Effect = {}]` loads clean, and the same law is still refused with the same two errors — because the gate keys on the OPERATION symbol's declared row, which is carrier-independent, while purity here is per-carrier. (Whether that binding is even RECORDED or silently dropped is unverified and worth checking on its own — proposal 045 says a closed effect is not a type argument.)\n\nWHAT WORKS TODAY, measured, and is the current repair the message names: declare the equation on a carrier that declares its OWN pure operations — a sort with `operation insert(...) -> ...` / `isEmpty(...)` and no effects clause carries `rule isEmpty(insert(?c, ?x)) <=> false [simp]` cleanly. The gap is that `List` does NOT declare its own `insert`/`isEmpty` — it inherits the spec's — so in the stdlib as it stands there is nowhere to put this law where it would fire.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the law above, declared on `PersistentCollection`, is accepted and FIRES for a pure carrier — driven, asserting the rewritten result, not that it loads; the same law still DECLINES for a carrier whose `insert` has a concrete effect, and that decline is LOUD, not a silent non-firing; the concretely-External case (054's own) keeps its load-time refusal — that is the control that this did not weaken the gate 054 asked for; `wi1049_effect_polymorphic_diagnostic_test` still passes or is updated with its reason; each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: proposal 054 'Consumers that must decline it — loudly' (WI-698/WI-702); WI-1049 (the classification and the message); WI-357/WI-365 (row grounding at the consumption boundary); WI-881 (an untagged equation is inert); proposal 045 (the row model)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "DESIGN NOTE (from the WI-1049 conversation) — THE FIRING SITE IS ALREADY IN THE TYPER, SO THE SUBSTITUTED ROW IS ALREADY IN HAND. This ticket's framing said the ingredient 'exists'; it is stronger than that. docs/design/simp-rewrite-design.md states the engine is 'type-directed, integrated with the typer' with 'the typer over op-body occurrences' as a call site (§1, §4.3), and §6 makes firing conditional on the RECEIVER'S SORT: 'a sort/spec rule applies iff min_sort(receiver) <: S (conformance, transitive)'. So at the moment a rewrite is considered, the carrier is known and sort-conformance is ALREADY being computed. The effect row sigma-instantiates off that same carrier. Checking the SUBSTITUTED row is therefore not a new mechanism at a new place — it is one more condition at a site that already resolves the carrier for a different reason.\n\nThat also answers 'where should this live': DURING TYPING, beside the existing conformance step — not at load-time formation (today), and not in the resolver. Note the doc's own §4.2 caveat: `apply_eq_rules` (resolve.rs) is the OTHER call site running the same rewriter over Term goals at compile time, and §4.4 says the semantic decisions belong to the SHARED CORE so both sites stay consistent. Any effect-row condition must go in that shared core, or the two sites will disagree — which is exactly the class of defect WI-1016/WI-860 record ('both carriers must key alike', 'two derivations must agree').\n\nSTILL OPEN, and the reason a brainstorm was asked for rather than a straight implementation: the relation between RULES and EFFECTS is not settled anywhere as a whole. The pieces are scattered — 054 'Consumers that must decline it' (formation-time refusal, argued for a CONCRETE External row), WI-881/CLAUDE.md ('[simp] is the enablement'; an untagged equation is inert), WI-818 ('a rule is a law, not backing'), WI-580 (an operation WITH a body derives its equational and relational views, so its rules are generated not written), and the row model (045, WI-357/WI-365 grounding at the consumption boundary). Questions they do not jointly answer: may a LAW (untagged, inert) mention an effectful operation at all, given it never rewrites? Does the answer differ for [simp] (rewrites, so duplicates/drops) versus [unfold] versus a bare law cited by `using` in a proof? Is an effect-polymorphic operation's law admissible as a CONDITIONAL equation (fires only where the row grounds to {}) — the shape 054 §3.4.1 already floats for `requires` ('carrying the dictionary as an equation antecedent is a future relaxation')? Settle those before writing the check, or the check will encode one answer by accident.", at: "2026-08-08T08:24:13Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "SHARPENING FROM THE BRAINSTORM (Q1) — 'THE INSTANTIATED ROW IS {}' AND 'THE INSTANTIATED ROW IS SAFE TO DROP A CALL FOR' ARE DIFFERENT CONDITIONS. This ticket proposes deciding the gate on the SUBSTITUTED row. Before implementing, note that emptiness is not the only axis, because effects are not uniform for this question:\n\n  * CONTROL effects — Error (`operation raise(error: T) -> Nothing`, and `sort Nothing` is UNINHABITED, so on the raising path there is NO VALUE), Branch (027: zero-or-many), non-termination. Rewriting `isEmpty(insert(?c,?x)) -> false` over an Error-carrying `insert` does not merely lose a side effect: it turns a program that RAISES into one that ANSWERS false. A WRONG ANSWER, not a lost effect.\n  * VALUE-PRESERVING effects — allocation, logging, Modify on unrelated state. The LHS always denotes; dropping the call loses the effect but not the answer.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TICKET, both directions:\n  * it makes the CURRENT refusal of the polymorphic case stronger, not weaker — a row VARIABLE can be instantiated to Error, so today's refusal is protecting against the control-effect case, not just being cautious about an effect running zero times;\n  * and it means a relaxation keyed on 'row grounds to {}' is the SOUND one, while a more permissive 'row contains no control effect' is NOT obviously sound for a rewrite that drops a call (the dropped effect still does not happen). Do not reach for the permissive form as an 'obvious' generalization.\n\nTHE HONEST TARGET for anyone who wants this law over an effectful carrier is a DIFFERENT EQUATION: the effect-preserving form — run the insert, then answer false — where both sides raise identically and nothing is dropped. Whether `<=>` can be given that sequencing reading is open (docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md Q1/Q6). The conditional shape is not a new mechanism either: guarded effect atoms already exist (`effects { s.E, Error[EmptyStream] :- isEmpty(s) }` on Stream.head / List.head), which is the same shape pointed the other way.\n\nSEE docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md — Q1 (which effects remove the value), Q3 (polymorphic as a third answer), Q4 (where the check goes, given firing is already type-directed), Q6 (what a law is FOR). Q6's dichotomy 'executable vs specification' gained a middle term from Q1: even as pure specification a law over a control-effecting op holds only on the defined path, so a law over an effectful operation needs a READING (total / partial / effect-preserving) before it needs a gate. Pick the reading first; what to check follows.", at: "2026-08-08T08:37:59Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "THE SURFACE CANNOT SPELL THE SOUND LAW — which narrows this ticket and moves the real request elsewhere. Worked out in docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md Q7/Q8.\n\nIn direct style `isEmpty(insert(c,x))` is not an application of isEmpty to a value; when insert is effectful it is `insert(c,x) >>= \\c' -> isEmpty(c')` — the bind is IMPLICIT IN THE NESTING. An effect operation is exactly one that may OMIT that bind: `raise` never invokes the continuation (`Err v >>= f = Err v`), `Branch` may invoke it zero or many times.\n\nAnd the matcher cannot reach it. simp-rewrite-design.md §'Matcher ≠ typer': 'a [simp] rule LHS is a functor-application pattern'. Matching `isEmpty(insert(?c, ?x))` binds ?c and ?x and NOT the continuation — in direct style the continuation is not a subterm, it is the nesting itself. So the RHS can only be built from ?c, ?x and constants, while the unconditionally-sound (fully effect-preserving) form needs to name `\\c' -> ...`. THE ONLY LAWS OF THIS SHAPE THE SYNTAX CAN EXPRESS ARE THE ONES THAT DISCARD THE BIND.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TICKET — it stays worth doing, and it stays narrow. Deciding the gate on the SUBSTITUTED row can only ever admit the case where the row grounds to {}, i.e. exactly where there is no bind to preserve. That is consistent with all of the above and is the whole of what a row check can buy. Do NOT let the ticket grow into 'admit laws over effectful operations' — that is not a gate change, it is a surface change (a way to write the bind, or a matcher that binds the continuation), and 047's reflect/reify is where it would start since that is the only place the monad is already spelled.\n\nALSO WORTH NAMING ONCE, because this ticket will touch the site: 054's three verbs are one property. DROPS = the bind is invoked zero times (Error, empty Branch); DUPLICATES = more than once (Branch); REORDERS = order-sensitive (state). So 'duplicates, reorders, or drops' is one condition — THE BIND IS INVOKED EXACTLY ONCE, IN PLACE — and it is the same condition 054's decline list needs for its other consumers ('any future memoization or CSE joins the same decline list'). If this ticket introduces a per-effect classification, classify on THAT property rather than enumerating verbs per consumer.", at: "2026-08-08T08:41:36Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "Q2 (does the tag matter?) — YES, and the axis is WHICH QUANTITY IS KNOWN WHERE. Recorded from the conversation; the proof case is the one with a concrete proposal attached.\n\n  * [simp] — fires DURING TYPING at a redex, so the carrier is known and the row is substitutable (Q4). But Q8: the rewrite must DROP the bind and the surface cannot spell the preserving form, so even with the row known the rewrite is sound only where the row is {} — i.e. where there is no bind to preserve.\n  * [unfold] — fired by the RESOLVER, not the typer. WI-757 measured exactly this asymmetry: keyed on `is_macro` alone, an effectful macro under [unfold] loaded clean and rewrote the effectful call into the program. Whether the carrier is known at that site is the open question for this arm, and it is NOT answered by [simp]'s answer.\n  * a bare law CITED IN A PROOF via `using` — NOTHING IS EXECUTED. 054's whole hazard is about a rewritten PROGRAM: duplicates/reorders/drops are properties of running the call. A proof step runs nothing, so that argument does not reach this use at all. What the proof use needs instead is only that the law be TRUE at the instantiation — and Q1 says a value equation over a CONTROL effect holds only on the defined path, while over an empty row it holds unconditionally.\n\nCONCRETE PROPOSAL for the proof arm: at the citation the carrier is concrete, so for `List` the row grounds to {} and the value equation is unconditionally true — so the citation is sound. Rather than checking that ad hoc, GENERATE THE OBLIGATION: emit an `Obligation` (anthill.realization; kernel-language.md §\"The kernel generates proof obligations — facts of entity type Obligation\") saying 'at this instantiation the row is {}', and let it be discharged at the usual trust levels. That turns the blanket formation refusal into a per-use, per-carrier obligation — which is the conditional-equation shape from Q3, discharged by the proof system instead of by a bespoke check. It also keeps the loud channel Q4 demands: an undischarged obligation is visible, where a silently-non-firing rewrite is not.\n\nVALUE OF A SMALL WORKED EXAMPLE, and it should be part of whatever lands: take the law on `List`, show the obligation generated, show it discharged, and show the same law over an effectful carrier producing an obligation that CANNOT be discharged. That is the non-vacuous form — it drives the mechanism rather than asserting a rule loaded.\n\nSTATE OF THE EXAMPLE, corrected by driving (this ticket's earlier text and my summary both had it wrong): `List` DOES declare its own `insert` — `operation insert(c: List, elem: T) -> List = cons(head: elem, tail: c)` at list.anthill:254, with a body and NO effects clause, so it is pure and passes the gate. What `List` does not declare is `isEmpty`; it imports `Stream.{isEmpty}`, whose row is `s.E` — the RECEIVER's effect parameter, projected. So the law placed inside `List` is refused ONCE, on `Stream.isEmpty` alone, not twice. WI-1049 has been extended to classify that path-dependent projection as Polymorphic rather than Effectful (it read the head only and so mis-called it), and the law is TRUE for List by construction — `insert` prepends a `cons`, so the result is never `nil`.", at: "2026-08-08T08:57:40Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "DECISION (2026-08-08) — DEFERRED, POST-RELEASE. Do not implement before release.\n\nQ3 is settled the other way from this ticket's framing: EFFECT-POLYMORPHIC => POTENTIALLY EFFECTFUL. Effect-polymorphism translates to effect. There may one day be effect-ELIMINATION rules that discharge the polymorphic case, but they are not known, and until they are, 'may be an effect' and 'is an effect' are ONE CATEGORY for every consumer that must decline. The union is named POTENTIALLY EFFECTFUL and that is what the gate refuses — one verdict, not two.\n\nWhat this ticket proposed (decide the gate on the SUBSTITUTED row) therefore survives only in one shape: A MODE THAT APPLIES [simp] AFTER TYPING, where effects are eliminated and the row is ground. That is the only place the polymorphic arm could earn a different verdict, and it is explicitly future work — NOT before release.\n\nALREADY LANDED, and it is the part that was worth doing now (WI-1049): the two cases are told apart in the DIAGNOSTIC while staying one verdict. `EquationBlock::Effectful` vs `::Polymorphic`, with the shared predicate named 'potentially effectful' in the message and the per-arm sentence explaining which and what to do. That was the actual defect — the gate called `PersistentCollection.insert` effectful when it is not — and it is fixed without touching the verdict. The classification also covers the path-dependent projection spelling (`effects s.E`, Stream.isEmpty).\n\nSO THE REMAINING SCOPE OF THIS TICKET IS THE POST-TYPING SIMP MODE, and nothing else. Everything about wording, classification and the s.E spelling is done. Whoever picks this up after release should re-read docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md first — in particular Q8, which argues the surface CANNOT SPELL the sound (effect-preserving) law at all, so even a post-typing mode can only admit the row-grounds-to-{} case. That bounds the payoff and should be weighed before building it.", at: "2026-08-08T09:04:46Z")
